<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wei Zhao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Center for Health Statistics and Information, National Health Commission People’s Republic of China, Beijing, 100044, China
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">ECG foundation models are increasingly popular due to their adaptability across various tasks. However, their clinical applicability is often limited by performance gaps compared to task-specific models, even after pre-training on large ECG datasets and fine-tuning on target data. This limitation is likely due to the lack of an effective post-training strategy. In this paper, we propose a simple yet effective post-training approach to enhance ECGFounder, a state-of-the-art ECG foundation model pre-trained on over 7 million ECG recordings. Experiments on the PTB-XL benchmark show that our approach improves the baseline fine-tuning strategy by 1.2%–3.3% in macro AUROC and 5.3%–20.9% in macro AUPRC. Additionally, our method outperforms several recent state-of-the-art approaches, including task-specific and advanced architectures. Further evaluation reveals that our method is more stable and sample-efficient compared to the baseline, achieving a 9.1% improvement in macro AUROC and a 34.9% improvement in macro AUPRC using just 10% of the training data. Ablation studies identify key components, such as stochastic depth and preview linear probing, that contribute to the enhanced performance. These findings underscore the potential of post-training strategies to improve ECG foundation models, and we hope this work will contribute to the continued development of foundation models in the ECG domain.</p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">#</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">#</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>These authors contributed equally.</span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Corresponding authors: Wei Zhao (zhaowei@nhc.gov.cn), Xiaohan Fan (fanxiaohan@fuwaihospital.org), Ya Zhou (zhouya@fuwai.com) </span></span></span><span id="footnotex3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Supervision: Wei Zhao</span></span></span>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p">Keywords: Electrocardiogram, Post-training strategy, ECGFounder, Model adaptation, Foundation models</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Electrocardiography (ECG) is a widely used, non-invasive technique for the screening, diagnosis, monitoring, and risk prediction of cardiovascular diseases. ECG captures the heart’s electrophysiological activity, offering valuable insights into cardiac function and health. In recent years, the rapid evolution of artificial intelligence, particularly deep learning, has revolutionized ECG analysis. Deep neural networks (DNNs) have demonstrated expert-level accuracy in detecting a wide range of cardiac abnormalities, such as arrhythmias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hannun et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. These models are increasingly deployed in clinical settings to assist cardiologists, alleviate diagnostic workloads, and enhance decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, DNNs have demonstrated the ability to identify subtle signal patterns undetectable by human experts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Somani et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siontis et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, enabling the diagnosis of conditions like structural heart disease <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poterucha et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, anemia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and diabetes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulkarni et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This has expanded the diagnostic potential of ECG far beyond its traditional role.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, foundation models have emerged as a transformative approach for building generalizable and transferable models across diverse clinical tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moor et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Trained on large-scale datasets, these models can be fine-tuned for various downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bommasani et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. In the ECG domain, foundation models such as ECG-FM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">McKeen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, HuBERT-ECG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and ECGFounder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have shown broad applicability across ECG-related tasks. The recently released foundation model, ECGFounder, built on the Harvard-Emory ECG Database <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koscova et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which includes over 10 million clinically annotated ECG recordings covering 150 types of cardiac abnormalities, has demonstrated strong performance and generalizability across multiple applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite significant advancements in ECG foundation models, substantial performance gaps persist when fine-tuned for specific diagnostic tasks, especially compared to recent task-specific models. For example, a recent study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>)</cite> reveals that HuBERT-ECG BASE, while performing well across several tasks on the PTB-XL benchmark, falls short when compared to those specialized models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Encouraged by the open-source efforts of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we conducted a series of experiments using the PTB-XL dataset. However, we observed that the fine-tuning performance of even the state-of-the-art foundation model ECGFounder remains suboptimal. This highlights a key issue: when ECG foundation models pre-trained on one large dataset are applied to ECG signals from other data sources, they often fail to match the performance of specialized models. Moreover, compared to recent advanced architectures—such as multi-scale models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and state-space models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2024</a>)</cite>—the performance of these foundation models lags significantly, raising concerns about their practical applicability in real-world clinical settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose a simple yet effective post-training strategy based on the recently released ECGFounder model. We use the PTB-XL all-label classification benchmark as a performance example. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, while pre-training on over 7 million ECG recordings brings substantial improvements over training from scratch, the benefits of pre-training are closely matched by our post-training approach, even when applied to randomly initialized models. When applied to the pre-trained ECGFounder, our proposed post-training strategy leads to remarkable performance gains of 3.2% in AUROC and 14.3% in AUPRC (Figures <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>). Furthermore, our experiments show that, compared to state-of-the-art task-specific models, our method achieves superior classification performance on several PTB-XL benchmark tasks. These results underscore the complementary advantages of the proposed post-training strategy, demonstrating its ability to address the performance gaps observed in ECG foundation models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12991v1/ECGFounder_AUROC_Comparison.svg" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/203;" width="219" height="203"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12991v1/ECGFounder_AUPRC_Comparison.svg" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/203;" width="219" height="203"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Performance comparison on PTB-XL 71-label classification benchmark. (a) Marco AUROC results comparing different training strategies. (b) Macro AUPRC results under the same experimental setup. “Random Initialization” denotes training from scratch, while “Pre-training” utilizes transferred weights. Both subfigures contrast baseline fine-tuning methods (left) with our proposed post-training approach (right).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed post-training strategy is grounded in two key insights. First, ECG signals inherently exhibit information redundancy, where neighboring time points and heartbeat cycles are often predictable from each other <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. To address this, we employ stochastic depth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2016</a>)</cite> to reduce redundancy and enhance the model’s robustness. Second, pre-training provides a valuable initialization that generally improves model performance. However, while the early layers of the model retain the pre-trained weights, the final classification head is typically initialized randomly. To optimize this, we introduce preview linear probing during post-training to better initialize the classification head, thereby boosting task-specific performance. Although these two components—stochastic depth and preview linear probing—are relatively simple, they play a crucial role in driving improvements in the performance of the ECG foundation model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To explore the effectiveness of the proposed post-training strategy, we conducted a series of experiments, including varying sample sizes and training length, and an ablation study. By evaluating performance under diverse conditions, we found that the strategy is both stable and sample-efficient, showing its capacity to enhance model performance not only with full datasets but also in scenarios with limited labeled data. These experiments provide valuable insights, demonstrating the promising performance and potential applicability of the method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the main contributions of this work are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1. </span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder</span>.
We propose a simple yet effective post-training strategy to enhance ECGFounder, a recently released state-of-the-art ECG foundation model. This strategy provides a crucial complement to the original framework, facilitating more efficient adaptation to diverse downstream ECG tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2. </span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Significant Performance Gains</span>.
Experiments on the PTB-XL benchmark demonstrate that our method outperforms the baseline fine-tuning strategy from the original work across multiple tasks, by 1.2%–3.3% in macro AUROC and 5.3%–20.9% in macro AUPRC. Furthermore, we explore the properties of the proposed method under varying sample sizes and training epochs. Notably, with only 10% of the training data, the proposed method achieves remarkable improvements of 9.1% in macro AUROC and 34.9% in macro AUPRC, highlighting its effectiveness in data-scarce scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Superior Performance</span>.
Our method surpasses several recent state-of-the-art task-specific and advanced models across multiple ECG classification tasks, highlighting the potential of ECG foundation models when equipped with an effective post-training strategy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical Implications</span>.
Recent studies have reported sub-optimal performance of fine-tuned ECG foundation models on the PTB-XL benchmark, raising concerns regarding their clinical applicability.
Our results suggest that this limitation likely stems from the absence of an effective post-training strategy, which could address the gap and enable real-world clinical adoption.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>ECG Classification and Evaluation Benchmarks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ECG classification is one of the most important tasks in ECG analysis, crucial for many critical applications, including the detection of arrhythmia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hannun et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hughes et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, structural heart disease <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poterucha et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, anemia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and diabetes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulkarni et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Due to their excellent performance in both accuracy and diagnostic speed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, deep learning methods have gained significant popularity in these tasks. Successful model architectures in these areas range from convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and Transformer to state-space models and ensemble approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Recent advanced architectures have proven effective in modeling the time-series nature of ECG signals, including multi-scale models like MSW-Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and MULTIRESNET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as well as advanced state-space models like SPACETIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Chimera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, comparing the performance of various ECG classification methods presents several challenges. While numerous large ECG datasets have been collected, many remain private <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Somani et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siontis et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Recent efforts to address this limitation have led to the release of several large, publicly available ECG datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clifford, 2024</a>)</cite>. Nonetheless, a fair comparison requires more than just raw datasets. Clearly defined usage guidelines, such as pre-defined train-test-splits, label sets, and target metrics, are essential to ensure valid comparisons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff, 2024</a>)</cite>. Among these, the PTB-XL dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wagner et al., 2020</a>)</cite> stands out as one of the most comprehensive benchmarks, providing essential data for evaluating various ECG classification methods. Furthermore, based on this dataset, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al. (2021)</a></cite> presents benchmark results for several methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ismail Fawaz et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, providing an important baseline for comparing different ECG classification algorithms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>ECG Foundation Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in foundation models for natural language processing have inspired similar approaches for health data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clifford, 2024</a>)</cite>. In the ECG domain, several studies have explored building foundation models through large-scale pre-training followed by evaluation across diverse downstream tasks. Representative examples include ECG-FM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">McKeen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, HuBERT-ECG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and ECGFounder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Specifically, ECG-FM is a Transformer-based model pre-trained on over 1 million 12-lead ECGs from multiple sources; HuBERT-ECG is another Transformer-based model pre-trained on more than 9 million 12-lead ECGs covering 164 cardiovascular conditions; and ECGFounder is a CNN-based model pre-trained on over 7 million 12-lead ECGs annotated with 150 diagnostic categories. Importantly, the codes and pre-trained model weights of all three models have been publicly released.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing the performance of different ECG foundation models would provide valuable insights for practitioners. However, conducting a fair comparison is challenging, especially for ECG foundation models due to their higher computational cost. As discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 ECG Classification and Evaluation Benchmarks ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the PTB-XL benchmark offers a fair platform for evaluating various algorithms. Nonetheless, the pre-training dataset for ECG-FM uses random splits of the PhysioNet 2021 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reyna et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldberger et al., 2000</a>)</cite>, which includes the PTB-XL dataset, potentially limiting its applicability for evaluation on the PTB-XL benchmark. For HuBERT-ECG, the authors have reported performance results on PTB-XL, enabling direct comparison. For ECGFounder, we follow the recommended fine-tuning strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> based on the released model weights to evaluate performance on the PTB-XL benchmark.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Regularization in Deep Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deep learning models often require significant capacity to capture complex representations, making them susceptible to overfitting, especially when the available training data is limited. Regularization is a widely used technique to mitigate overfitting and improve model generalization. In general, regularization refers to any technique applied in the model architecture, learning process, or inference to alleviate the limitations of insufficient training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moradi et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. A common regularization method is weight decay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hanson and Pratt, 1988</a>)</cite>, which penalizes large weights during each update step. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter (2019)</a></cite> demonstrated that weight decay, combined with a cosine annealing learning rate schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017</a>)</cite>, benefits optimizers like Adam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma and Ba, 2014</a>)</cite>. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky et al. (2012)</a></cite> showed that weight decay, when combined with Dropout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srivastava et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, reduces overfitting effectively. In addition, stochastic depth is another regularization technique designed to address challenges like vanishing gradients, diminishing feature reuse, and long training times in deep networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. Recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite> demonstrated that stochastic depth is also effective in reducing information redundancy in ECG signals, improving performance on various ECG classification tasks. We explore these strategies in our study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following the general definition of regularization, initialization strategies can be considered a form of regularization. The pre-training paradigm, which has been successful in many ECG tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">McKeen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, retains the pre-trained weights in the early layers of the model. However, the final classification head is typically initialized randomly. In this paper, we use linear probing to initialize this layer. Furthermore, early stopping can also be regarded as a regularization technique, and it is often used alongside other regularization methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. We incorporate early stopping in our experiments as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we propose a post-training strategy designed to enhance the classification performance of ECGFounder, a recently released state-of-the-art ECG foundation model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The overall framework of the proposed approach is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Our framework builds upon the publicly available network architecture and pre-trained weights of ECGFounder, and introduces two key stages for downstream classification: an initialization stage and a regularization stage. The design and implementation of each stage are described in detail in the following subsections.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12991v1/framework.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/127;" width="381" height="127"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of the proposed post-training framework for ECG classification.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview of ECGFounder</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection provides a concise overview of ECGFounder. The model is built upon the CNN-based Net1D architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, adapted from RegNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radosavovic et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and specifically optimized for ECG signal analysis. Its design incorporates stagewise scaling, bottleneck blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, and channel-wise attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, enabling the model to capture both temporal dynamics and inter-lead spatial dependencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For pre-training, ECGFounder was built on part of the Harvard-Emory ECG Database <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koscova et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which contains over 10 million ECGs across 150 diagnostic categories, using a multilabel setting that reflects the complexity of real-world clinical practice. To address incomplete annotations, a positive–unlabeled (PU) learning strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> was integrated into the loss function, allowing the model to account for unlabeled but potentially positive samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For downstream tasks, ECGFounder supports both linear probing and full fine-tuning, as proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>. These training strategies are optimized to ensure robust performance across diverse ECG applications. Specifically, training is guided by a ReduceLROnPlateau learning rate scheduler with the AdamW optimizer. Model selection is based on validation macro Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC), aiming to enhance adaptability and reliability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Post-training Strategy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our method builds upon ECGFounder and introduces a two-stage post-training strategy. The strategy is designed to enhance classification performance while requiring minimal modifications to the original model architecture. Specifically, it consists of an initialization stage and a regularization stage, which are described in detail below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Initialization Stage</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The initialization stage follows a standard linear probing approach. Specifically, all pretrained backbone parameters are frozen, and the linear classification head is replaced with a task-specific counterpart trained from scratch. This design allows the final layer to be rapidly aligned with the pretrained representations while providing a well-conditioned initialization for downstream classification. As discussed in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, an appropriate initialization can facilitate task-specific adaptation and ultimately improve model performance. Following the general definition of regularization presented in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, this step can also be viewed as a form of regularization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Regularization Stage</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The regularization stage involves full fine-tuning of the entire model following initialization, with multiple regularization techniques incorporated to improve generalization. The core component is the stochastic depth strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, which has been shown to effectively reduce redundancy in ECG signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Specifically, stochastic depth is applied to all residual connections in ECGFounder. With a predefined drop-path rate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, a binary random mask <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\mathbb{I}(U&lt;1-\lambda),\quad U\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\mathbb{I}(U&lt;1-\lambda),\quad U\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the uniform distribution and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the input tensor, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the block transformation, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> the block output. The stochastic depth formulation during training is expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{X}}=\frac{r}{1-\lambda}\cdot F(\mathbf{X})+\mathbf{X}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{X}}=\frac{r}{1-\lambda}\cdot F(\mathbf{X})+\mathbf{X},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> determines whether the transformation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is active <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r=1)</annotation></semantics></math> or skipped <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r=0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the scaling factor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1-\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1-\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that the expected output is preserved. During inference, the deterministic residual form is used:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{X}}=F(\mathbf{X})+\mathbf{X}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{X}}=F(\mathbf{X})+\mathbf{X}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further stabilize training, Dropout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srivastava et al., 2014</a>)</cite> is applied to the activated outputs within all bottleneck blocks. In addition, a cosine annealing learning rate schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017</a>)</cite> and weight decay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hanson and Pratt, 1988</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019</a>)</cite> are employed to provide smooth decay and enhance robustness during optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Ease of Implementation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve the simplicity and accessibility of ECGFounder, we deliberately minimize modifications to the original framework. All proposed strategies avoid deep architectural changes and can be readily integrated into the existing implementation. In the initialization stage, our method can be directly applied within the open-source ECGFounder code. In the regularization stage, Dropout, weight decay the cosine annealing learning-rate schedule are standard functions in PyTorch. The stochastic depth operation is also straightforward to implement, requiring only a single-line modification within the bottleneck block:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">out += identity</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is replaced with</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">out = self.drop_path(out) + identity</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">out</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">identity</span> are tensors already defined in the implementation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, while <span id="S3.SS3.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">self.drop_path()</span> represents the stochastic depth strategy employed in this work. Overall, the proposed post-training strategy remains simple yet effective, preserving the ease of use of ECGFounder while providing enhanced performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a comprehensive set of experiments conducted on the PTB-XL benchmark dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. We first describe the datasets and the experimental settings. We then evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed post-training strategy by comparing its performance against the suggested fine-tuning strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and several state-of-the-art methods, including recent ECG foundation models and task-specific approaches that have been carefully optimized. Additionally, we conduct experiments to further investigate the properties of the proposed method, including performance comparisons under varying training epochs and sample sizes, as well as an ablation study.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PTB-XL database is a widely used open-source dataset for evaluating ECG model performance, notable for its relatively large sample size, high-quality annotations and clearly defined usage guidelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wagner et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff, 2024</a>)</cite>. It contains 21,837 clinical 12-lead ECG recordings, each 10 seconds in duration, collected from 18,885 patients across a broad age range (0–95 years). Each recording was annotated by up to two cardiologists, with labels drawn from a set of 71 distinct ECG statements. These statements can be organized into 12 rhythm labels and 44 diagnostic labels, which can further be hierarchically grouped into 24 subclasses. Multiple labels may be associated with a single recording, reflecting the inherently multi-label nature of clinical ECG interpretation. Based on this labeling system, we consider four multi-label classification tasks: (1) all-71, which uses the complete set of 71 labels; (2) rhythm-12, which focuses on 12 rhythm categories; (3) diagnostic-44, which focuses on 44 diagnostic categories; and (4) subclass-23, which focuses on 23 hierarchical subclasses derived from the diagnostic labels. As discussed in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, PTB-XL provides predefined splits into training, validation, and testing sets (approximately 8:1:1), enabling consistent and fair comparisons across methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff, 2024</a>)</cite>. We follow this standard data splitting in all experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the initialization stage, we follow the training recipe recommended in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, except for a shorter training length, since the largest validation macro Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC) is typically achieved in the early epochs. Specifically, the model is trained for 30 epochs with a batch size of 256, using AdamW with a weight decay of 0.1 and an initial learning rate of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. A ReduceLROnPlateau scheduler (maximization mode) monitors macro AUROC on the validation set and reduces the learning rate by a factor of 0.1 if no improvement is observed for 10 consecutive epochs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the regularization stage, we also train for 30 epochs with a batch size of 256. Optimization is performed using AdamW with a weight decay of 0.05 and a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. A CosineAnnealingLR scheduler is adopted with a minimum learning rate of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, we apply a droppath rate of 0.5 and a dropout rate of 0.05.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For both stages, the model is evaluated on the validation set at the end of each epoch, and the checkpoint with the highest macro AUROC is selected for final evaluation. This strategy serves as a form of early stopping regularization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Comparison with Other Methods</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents a comprehensive comparison between the proposed post-training method and a range of baseline approaches, including the suggested baseline fine-tuning strategy, referred to ECGFounder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. To distinguish the post-training variant, we denote our method as ECGFounder-PT (Post-Training). Additional baselines include the foundation model HuBERT-ECG-BASE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and task-specific and advanced models, such as inception1d <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ismail Fawaz et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, resnet1d101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, resnet1d_wang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, ensemble-7-models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, SPACETIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, MSW-Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, MULTIRESNET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and Chimera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The reported performance values are derived from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
