<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Performance comparison in terms of macro AUROC. Best and second-best results are highlighted in bold and underlined, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Comparison with Other Methods ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the macro AUROC of all methods, along with 95% confidence intervals computed using 1000 bootstrapped samples. ECGFounder-PT achieves the highest AUROC on three tasks: all-71 (0.944), subclass-23 (0.938), and rhythm-12 (0.979), and the second-highest on diagnostic-44 (0.946). Compared with the original ECGFounder and HuBERT-ECG-BASE, ECGFounder-PT improves performance by 1.2%–3.3% and 2.3%-4.5%, respectively, demonstrating the effectiveness of the post-training adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to foundation models, ECGFounder-PT also outperforms several recent state-of-the-art task-specific approaches. Compared to CNN-based baselines (inception1d, resnet1d101, resnet1d_wang, and ensemble-7-models) from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al. (2021)</a></cite>, ECGFounder-PT yields macro AUROC gains of 0.7%–3.5%.When compared to multi-scale architectures like MULTIRESNET and MSW-Transformer, which explicitly exploit the hierarchical properties of ECG signals, it improves performance by 0.4%–5.8%. Even compared with recent state space models (SPACETIME and Chimera), ECGFounder-PT achieves consistent gains of 0.3%–1.2%, except for diagnostic-44, where Chimera slightly outperforms it by 0.1%. Collectively, these results highlight the robustness of ECGFounder-PT and its ability to bridge the adaptation gap of foundation models, achieving balanced generalization across diverse ECG classification tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although AUROC is a standard benchmark metric, it may be less sensitive in the presence of class imbalance. To address this, we additionally report macro Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC) in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Comparison with Other Methods ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, with 95% confidence intervals computed using 1000 bootstrapped samples. Since AUPRC results for other methods are not available, the comparison is limited to ECGFounder and ECGFounder-PT. Across all tasks, ECGFounder-PT consistently outperforms ECGFounder, with relative gains ranging from 5.3% to 20.9%, underscoring the effectiveness of the post-training strategy, particularly in imbalanced data scenarios.</p>
</div>
