<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Comparison of AUROC and AUPRC between the baseline training recipe and the proposed post-training strategy across epochs on PTB-XL validation and testing sets.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Performance with Varying Training Sample Sizes</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5 Performance with Varying Training Sample Sizes ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares the proposed post-training method (ECGFounder-PT) with the baseline fine-tuning strategy (ECGFounder) on the PTB-XL all-71 task, using varying training sample sizes from 10% to 100% of the available data. Macro AUROC and macro AUPRC are used as evaluation metrics. As in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Performance with Varying Training Epochs ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, since validation and test sets exhibit similar patterns, we present results from the testing set for clarity. This analysis explores the applicability of the proposed method in real-world scenarios where the availability of training data varies across medical institutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, ECGFounder-PT consistently outperforms ECGFounder across all sample sizes, with improvements ranging from 3.0% to 9.1% in macro AUROC and 10.7% to 34.9% in macro AUPRC (Figures <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.5 Performance with Varying Training Sample Sizes ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4.sf4" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.5 Performance with Varying Training Sample Sizes ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(d)</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The largest performance gains occur under limited data conditions. With only 10% of the training set, ECGFounder-PT achieves a macro AUROC of 0.899 compared to 0.824 for ECGFounder (+9.1%) and a macro AUPRC of 0.294 compared to 0.218 (+34.9%). These results demonstrate the substantial advantage of the post-training strategy in data-scarce environments, a common challenge in clinical practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As training data increases, the relative improvements decrease but remain consistent. For instance, at 30% of the training data, ECGFounder-PT achieves an AUROC of 0.925 and an AUPRC of 0.364, outperforming ECGFounder’s 0.877 and 0.301 (+5.5% and +21.0%, respectively). Even at higher data availability (e.g., 90%), ECGFounder-PT maintains an edge, reaching 0.942 AUROC and 0.402 AUPRC compared to 0.915 and 0.363, corresponding to relative gains of 2.9% and 10.7%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These findings highlight the robustness of ECGFounder-PT: the post-training strategy yields consistent performance gains across diverse data regimes, with the most pronounced benefits under data scarcity. This property underscores its practical value for real-world deployment across diverse healthcare environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12991v1/figure_a_val_auroc.svg" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/201;" width="219" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12991v1/figure_b_test_auroc.svg" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/201;" width="219" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12991v1/figure_c_val_auprc.svg" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/201;" width="219" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12991v1/figure_d_test_auprc.svg" id="S4.F4.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/201;" width="219" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Comparison of AUROC and AUPRC between the baseline training recipe and the proposed post-training strategy across varying training sample sizes on PTB-XL validation and testing sets.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the contribution of each component in the proposed post-training strategy, we conducted an ablation study on the PTB-XL all-71 task. The results are reported in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.6 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In this analysis, “random initialization” refers to training the model from scratch with default weight initialization. For all other cases, the initialization was derived from ECGFounder, which was pre-trained on more than 7 million ECG recordings. “without droppath” indicates the removal of the stochastic depth operation, while “without linear probing” excludes the preview linear probing initialization. Likewise, “without dropout” and “without cosine schedule” denote the exclusion of dropout regularization and cosine annealing learning rate decay, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.6 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, every component in the proposed strategy contributes to the overall performance improvement. Among them, the stochastic depth operation and the preview linear probing initialization are the most important. Removing either of these components results in substantial performance degradation: the AUPRC decreases by approximately 4.1%–5.1% on the validation set and 0.7%–4.4% on the testing set, while AUROC decreases by 0.3%–0.4% and 1.1%–1.6% on the validation and testing sets, respectively. In contrast, the absence of dropout or cosine learning rate schedule leads to only marginal differences, suggesting that these techniques primarily provide auxiliary benefits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is also noteworthy that, even under random initialization, the proposed post-training strategy achieves performance comparable to the baseline strategy that leverages pre-training on more than 7 million ECGs. This demonstrates the robustness of the proposed approach and underscores its potential value for advancing ECG foundation models.</p>
</div>
