</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">1. Performance Gains on ECG Foundation Models</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we evaluated the effectiveness of the proposed post-training strategy on four PTB-XL multi-label ECG classification benchmark tasks. The proposed method demonstrates consistent improvements over recent open-source ECG foundation models, confirming its ability to enhance generalization across diverse ECG classification tasks. The macro AUROC values of 0.944 (all-71), 0.946 (diagnostic-44), 0.938 (subclass-23), and 0.979 (rhythm-12) indicate 1.2%–3.3% gains over the baseline fine-tuning strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and 2.3%-4.5% over another foundation model HuBERT-ECG-BASE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Improvements in the macro AUPRC, ranging from 5.3% to 20.9%, compared to the baseline fine-tuning strategy, further highlight the model’s robustness in the context of imbalanced clinical datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">2. Comparison with State-of-the-Art</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that the proposed method consistently outperforms task-specific and advanced architectures. Baseline methods such as inception1d, xresnet1d101, resnet1d_wang, and ensemble-7-models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>)</cite> exhibit competitive or even superior performance to recent ECG foundation models, likely due to their specialized design for ECG signals. Similarly, the multi-scale architecture MULTIRESNET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>)</cite> performs well, reflecting the inherent multi-scale structure in ECG data, while advanced models like SPACETIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Chimera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behrouz et al., 2024</a>)</cite> highlight the significance of carefully designed architectures for ECG time-series data. These task-specific and advanced models reveal a deeper understanding of ECG signals, as they are tailored for the specific challenges of ECG classification. In contrast, foundation models like ECGFounder and HuBERT-ECG-BASE prioritize broad adaptability, which may not rely on a strong inductive bias specific to some ECG tasks. This flexibility allows foundation models to be applied across a wide range of tasks, but in certain cases, it can result in lower performance compared to specialized models, especially in the absence of an appropriate post-training strategy. As demonstrated in both our study and the work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al. (2024)</a></cite>, foundation models often lag behind task-specific and advanced architectures, even when pre-trained on large ECG datasets. This performance gap underscores the critical need for a robust post-training strategy. Our results, shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Comparison with Other Methods ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, clearly illustrate that incorporating an effective post-training approach significantly enhances the performance of ECG foundation models, enabling them to match or even surpass task-specific and advanced architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">3. Sample Efficiency and Limited Data Scenarios</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed post-training strategy demonstrates substantial benefits across a wide range of sample sizes, with particularly significant improvements in scenarios where data is limited. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5 Performance with Varying Training Sample Sizes ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridging Performance Gaps for Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy for ECGFounder" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, when trained with only 10% of the dataset, approximately 1,700 ECG samples, the proposed method achieves a macro AUROC of 0.899 and a macro AUPRC of 0.294, compared to 0.824 and 0.218 for the baseline strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, resulting in relative improvements of 9.1% and 34.9%, respectively. Even with 90% of the training data, the post-training strategy continues to deliver consistent gains, with macro AUROC and AUPRC improvements of 2.9% and 10.7%, respectively. These findings highlight the effectiveness of the post-training approach in reducing the dependency on large annotated datasets, which is particularly beneficial in clinical ECG applications where labeled data is often scarce. This ability to enhance model performance with limited data underscores the practical utility of the proposed strategy in real-world medical settings, where access to extensive labeled ECG datasets may be constrained.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">4. Training Stability and Convergence</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key factor driving the observed improvements is the enhanced training stability and convergence behavior facilitated by the proposed post-training strategy. Specifically, the method converges rapidly, surpassing a macro AUROC of 0.940 by epoch 8 and stabilizing at a plateau around 0.944 for the remainder of the training. In contrast, the baseline strategy exhibits slower convergence, with AUROC oscillating between 0.901 and 0.919 from epochs 16 to 30. Similarly, macro AUPRC reaches a stable value of 0.435, representing an 18.2% relative improvement over the baseline. These findings indicate that the post-training strategy effectively regularizes the optimization process, steering the model towards a more generalizable solution while mitigating overfitting. This enhanced stability not only accelerates convergence but also contributes to the overall robustness of the model, ensuring better performance across varying training conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">5. Ablation Study Insights</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ablation study highlights the contribution of individual components to overall performance. Stochastic depth and preview linear probing emerged as the most influential factors, with their removal leading to a decrease in macro AUPRC by 4.1%–5.1% on the validation set and 0.7%–4.4% on the testing set. The importance of stochastic depth has also been confirmed in a recent study on ECG classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, underscoring its role in enhancing generalization. Preview linear probing, on the other hand, provides better initialization for the classification head, thereby boosting performance as expected. Conceptually, this strategy resonates with initialization approaches in statistical learning. For example, motivated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2013)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> propose a sequential downsizing initialization strategy for tensor regression, which essentially performs lightweight training to induce a bias aligned with the target data. Although theoretical understanding of deep learning still lags behind its empirical success, insights from traditional statistical models may inspire more principled approaches to enhance modern deep learning methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">6. Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another important observation pertains to the performance metrics used. While macro AUROC remains the standard metric in ECG benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strodthoff et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, it is relatively insensitive to class imbalance, which is a common characteristic of clinical ECG datasets. In contrast, macro AUPRC is more sensitive to this imbalance, as it captures the precision-recall trade-off <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ozenne et al., 2015</a>)</cite>. This distinction is evident in most of our experiments. For instance, in the four PTB-XL multi-label tasks examined in this study, the proposed method achieves improvements of 1.2%–3.3% in macro AUROC compared to the baseline, whereas the improvement in macro AUPRC ranges from 5.3% to 20.9%. On the one hand, the substantial gains in macro AUPRC highlight the efficacy of the post-training strategy. On the other hand, the notable discrepancy between macro AUROC and AUPRC underscores an important insight: even a small difference in macro AUROC can correspond to significant variations in macro AUPRC, indicating that different methods may exhibit considerable differences in their ability to detect true positives in the presence of a large number of negatives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">7. Implications and Future Directions</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings have important implications for the design and clinical deployment of ECG foundation models.
While recent successes of foundation models in natural language processing have inspired similar approaches for health data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clifford, 2024</a>)</cite>, ECG foundation models face unique challenges due to the domain-specific characteristics of ECG signals, such as high information redundancy and physiological variability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Recent studies have reported that existing ECG foundation models achieve sub-optimal performance on the PTB-XL benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Coppola et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, raising concerns regarding their direct clinical applicability.
Our results indicate that this limitation may be attributed to the absence of an effective post-training strategy.
By addressing this gap with our proposed post-training strategy, the ECG foundation model can achieve strong performance on downstream tasks.
This highlights the critical role of post-training in enabling real-world clinical adoption of foundation models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we propose a simple yet effective post-training strategy for the recently released ECG foundation model, ECGFounder. Experiments on the PTB-XL benchmark demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms both the baseline fine-tuning strategy and several recent state-of-the-art models. Key components, including stochastic depth and preview linear probing, were identified as critical drivers of performance improvement. Additionally, the proposed method exhibits greater sample efficiency and training stability. Our findings underscore the potential of post-training strategies to bridge the adaptation gap for ECG foundation models, highlighting a promising direction to further advance their development.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography">References</h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
